## How Do I Install Codex?

### Package Managers

    # npm (recommended)
    npm install -g @openai/codex

    # Homebrew (macOS)
    brew install --cask codex

    # winget (Windows)
    winget install OpenAI.Codex

    # Upgrade to latest
    npm install -g @openai/codex@latest

### How Do I Update, Downgrade, or Uninstall the Codex CLI?

Codex ships a self-update command as of v0.128.0; everything else runs through whichever
package manager installed it.[86](#fn:163)

    # Check what you are running
    codex --version                          # -> codex-cli 0.145.0
    npm view @openai/codex version           # Latest published version

    # Update
    codex update                             # Built-in self-update (v0.128.0+)
    npm install -g @openai/codex@latest      # Or update via npm
    brew upgrade --cask codex                # Or via Homebrew

    # Pin or downgrade to an exact version
    npm install -g @openai/[[email protected]](/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection)

    # Uninstall
    npm uninstall -g @openai/codex
    brew uninstall --cask codex
    winget uninstall OpenAI.Codex

**Pinning is the right move when a release breaks you.** Codex tracks a fast stable line, so
installing an exact version is the fastest rollback – file the issue, pin, and move on.
Verify the rollback took with `codex --version`, since a shadowed second install on your `PATH` will silently keep serving the old binary (`codex doctor` flags exactly this under **Environment**).
> **Uninstalling the package does not remove your state.** Auth, config, and session history
> live in `~/.codex/`. Leave it in place to preserve logins and history across a reinstall;
> delete it only for a genuinely clean slate – `rm -rf ~/.codex` also discards `config.toml`, `auth.json`, and every stored session.

### Direct Install Script (v0.106.0+)

For macOS and Linux, a one-line install script is available as a GitHub release asset:[60](#fn:62)

    curl -fsSL https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/latest/download/install.sh | sh

The script auto-detects your platform and architecture, downloads the correct binary, and places it on your PATH.

### Binary Downloads

For environments without npm or Homebrew, download platform-specific binaries from [GitHub Releases](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases)[1](#fn:1):

| Platform | Binary |
| --- | --- |
| macOS Apple Silicon | `codex-aarch64-apple-darwin.tar.gz` |
| macOS x86_64 | `codex-x86_64-apple-darwin.tar.gz` |
| Linux x86_64 | `codex-x86_64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz` |
| Linux arm64 | `codex-aarch64-unknown-linux-musl.tar.gz` |

### System Requirements

- **macOS**: Apple Silicon or Intel (full sandbox support via Seatbelt)
- **Linux**: x86_64 or arm64 (sandbox via Landlock + seccomp)
- **Windows**: Native sandbox with restricted tokens (promoted from experimental in v0.100.0). WSL also supported[2](#fn:2)

### Authentication

    codex login                  # Interactive OAuth (recommended)
    codex login --device-auth    # OAuth device code flow (headless)
    codex login --with-api-key   # API key from stdin
    codex login status           # Check auth state (exit 0 = logged in)
    codex logout                 # Clear stored credentials

Two authentication paths:

1. **ChatGPT Account** (recommended): Sign in with your existing Plus, Pro, Team, Business, Edu, or Enterprise subscription. Full feature access including cloud tasks.
2. **API Key**: Set via `CODEX_API_KEY` environment variable or `codex login --with-api-key`. Some features (cloud threads) may be unavailable.

> **Expert tip**: Credential storage is configurable via `cli_auth_credentials_store` in `config.toml`. Options: `file` (default), `keyring` (OS keychain), or `auto` (keyring if available, file fallback).

**Amazon Bedrock login (v0.145.0, experimental):** Teams whose model access runs through Amazon Bedrock get an experimental login path with custom endpoint and authentication support, alongside the existing managed API-key authentication (v0.140.0+) and the AWS credential chain (see Custom Model Providers). GPT-5.6 Sol is the default Bedrock model.[112](#fn:194)

### Shell Completions

    # Generate completions for your shell
    codex completion bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/codex
    codex completion zsh > ~/.zsh/completions/_codex
    codex completion fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/codex.fish

### Verify Installation

    codex --version
    # codex-cli 0.133.0

