## Which Model Should I Choose?

### Available Models (April 2026)

| Model | Input / Total Context | Default Reasoning | Best For |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| **gpt-5.5** (Codex) | 400K / 400K | `medium` | **New flagship (April 23, 2026)** — 82.7% Terminal-Bench 2.0 SOTA; recommended default for most Codex tasks. **In API**: 1M context window.[83](#fn:160) |
| **gpt-5.5-pro** | 1M / 1M | `high` | Highest-effort tier on GPT-5.5 (April 24, 2026, API-available)[83](#fn:160) |
| **gpt-5.4** | 1M / 1M | `medium` | Prior flagship; bundled GPT-5.4 selections migrate to the GPT-5.6 Terra/Luna variants in v0.145.0[112](#fn:194) |
| **gpt-5.4-mini** | 400K / 400K | `medium` | Subagent work, simpler tasks — 30% of GPT-5.4 quota, 2x faster; superseded as the bundled pick by the GPT-5.6 variants in v0.145.0[76](#fn:81)[112](#fn:194) |
| **gpt-5.6 Sol / Terra / Luna** | 272K / 272K | — (`max` supported) | GPT-5.6 family — Bedrock-hosted since v0.143.0; v0.145.0 makes Sol the default Bedrock model and migrates bundled GPT-5.4 selections to Terra/Luna. Context windows corrected to 272,000 tokens in v0.144.6[106](#fn:188)[111](#fn:193)[112](#fn:194) |
| **gpt-5.3-codex** | 272K / 400K | `medium` | Coding specialist: complex software engineering |
| **gpt-5.3-codex-spark** | 128K / 128K | `high` | Near-instant iteration, text-only (Pro users, Cerebras partnership)[67](#fn:69) |
| **gpt-5.2-codex** | 272K / 400K | `medium` | Shut down July 23, 2026 per OpenAI’s deprecation table; recommended substitute is now `gpt-5.6-sol`[88](#fn:165) |
| **gpt-5.1-codex-mini** | 272K / 400K | `medium` | Shut down July 23, 2026 per OpenAI’s deprecation table; recommended substitute is now `gpt-5.6-terra`[88](#fn:165) |

> **GPT-5.5 (April 23, 2026)** is OpenAI’s recommended choice for most Codex tasks: complex coding, computer use, knowledge work, and research workflows. Available in Codex CLI / web / desktop on April 23 for ChatGPT Plus / Pro / Business / Enterprise / Edu / Go; in the OpenAI API on April 24. **Context window: 400K in Codex, 1M in the API** — Codex caps the window at 400K to balance throughput and cost across subscriber tiers; the API exposes the full 1M. Pricing (API): **$5 input / $30 output per MTok** (2× the GPT-5.4 rate; OpenAI states ~20% effective increase after token-efficiency improvements). Benchmarks: **82.7% Terminal-Bench 2.0** (current SOTA across publicly available models), 84.9% GDPval (44 occupations), 78.7% OSWorld-Verified, 98.0% Tau2-bench Telecom (no prompt tuning). OpenAI used GPT-5.5 + Codex internally to rewrite serving infrastructure pre-launch — yielded a 20% boost in token generation speed.[83](#fn:160)
>
> GPT-5.4 stays available across all Codex surfaces (CLI, app, IDE extension, cloud).[64](#fn:66) The exact model list varies by account and rollout. Check your local cache: `~/.codex/models_cache.json`.
>
> **Deprecation note (March 11, 2026)**: GPT-5.1 models are no longer available in ChatGPT. Existing conversations automatically continue on GPT-5.3 Instant, GPT-5.4 Thinking, or GPT-5.4 Pro. GPT-5.1-Codex-Mini remains available via API and CLI for cost-sensitive workloads.[71](#fn:73)
>
> **Free-tier note (May 5, 2026)**: GPT-5.5 Instant rolled out to the ChatGPT free tier on May 5, 2026. This widens the audience for the GPT-5.5 family beyond paid plans, though Codex CLI access continues to require an eligible Plus / Pro / Business / Enterprise / Edu / Go subscription or API key.[92](#fn:169)
>
> **GPT-5.4 mini (March 17, 2026)**: A smaller, faster variant of GPT-5.4 with 400K context at $0.75/$4.50 per MTok — uses only 30% of GPT-5.4 quota. Ideal for subagent delegation: let GPT-5.4 handle planning and coordination while GPT-5.4 mini subagents handle narrower subtasks (codebase search, file review, document processing) in parallel.[76](#fn:81)
>
> **Bedrock GPT-5.6 (Sol, Terra, Luna) (v0.143.0, July 8, 2026)**: Codex added first-class support for three GPT-5.6 model variants hosted on Amazon Bedrock, including support for the `max` reasoning-effort level. For teams whose model access runs through a Bedrock provider, these are selectable the same way as other custom-provider models (see Custom Model Providers); the `max` effort tier targets the hardest reasoning and agentic workloads.[106](#fn:188) **v0.144.6 (July 18, 2026)** refreshed the bundled instructions for Sol, Terra, and Luna and corrected their context windows to **272,000 tokens**.[111](#fn:193) **v0.145.0 (July 21, 2026)** adds experimental Bedrock login with custom endpoint and authentication support, makes **GPT-5.6 Sol the default Bedrock model**, and migrates bundled GPT-5.4 model selections to the corresponding **Terra and Luna** variants.[112](#fn:194)

### Model Selection Flowchart

    Is this a quick fix or simple question?
    ├─ Yes → gpt-5.1-codex-mini (fastest, cheapest)
    └─ No
       ├─ Do you need real-time pairing speed?
       │  ├─ Yes → gpt-5.3-codex-spark (near-instant, Pro only)
       │  └─ No
       │     ├─ Subagent or parallel subtask (search, review, processing)?
       │     │  ├─ Yes → gpt-5.4-mini (30% of GPT-5.4 quota, 2x faster)
       │     │  └─ No
       │     │     ├─ Pure coding task (refactor, migration, feature build)?
       │     │     │  ├─ Yes → gpt-5.3-codex (coding specialist, 272K context)
       │     │     │  └─ No → gpt-5.5 (new flagship: 400K context in Codex / 1M in API, 82.7% Terminal-Bench 2.0 SOTA)
       └─ Still unsure? → gpt-5.5

### Reasoning Effort

Control how much the model “thinks” before responding:

| Level | Behavior | When to Use |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `minimal` | Minimal reasoning (GPT-5 models only) | Trivial tasks, quick lookups |
| `low` | Brief reasoning | Standard coding tasks, formatting |
| `medium` | Balanced (default) | Most development work |
| `high` | Extended reasoning | Complex bugs, architecture |
| `xhigh` | Maximum reasoning | Security audits, deep analysis |

> Supported levels are model-dependent. `minimal` is only available for GPT-5 models. Not all models support every level.

    codex -c model_reasoning_effort="xhigh" "find the race condition"
> **Expert tip**: `xhigh` reasoning can use 3-5x more tokens than `medium` for the same prompt. Reserve it for genuinely hard problems where the extra thinking pays off.

**TUI quick reasoning controls (v0.124.0+).**[85](#fn:162) In an interactive TUI session, **`Alt+,`** lowers reasoning one step and **`Alt+.`** raises it one step — useful when a hard problem mid-session warrants a temporary `medium → high → xhigh` ramp without `/effort` or `-c`. When you accept a model upgrade mid-session, reasoning resets to the new model’s default rather than carrying the prior level over.

### Model Switching

Switch models mid-session with the `/model` slash command, or set per-run via `--model` / `-m`:

    codex -m gpt-5.3-codex-spark "pair with me on this component"

