## How Do I Debug Issues?

### Common Problems and Solutions

| Problem | Cause | Solution |
| --- | --- | --- |
| “Re-connecting” loop | Multiple Codex instances | Kill all processes, wait 60s, restart single instance |
| 401 auth errors | Stale credentials | `rm ~/.codex/auth.json && codex login` |
| Network blocked in sandbox | Default behavior | `-c 'sandbox_workspace_write.network_access=true'` |
| WSL2 disconnections | WSL state corruption | `wsl --shutdown` in PowerShell, wait 1 min, restart |
| Patch failures | Line ending mismatches | Normalize to LF, provide exact file text |
| Context compacting fails | Too much context | Reduce reasoning effort, split into smaller tasks |
| Model changes unexpectedly | Config.toml override | Run `/config` to inspect effective settings and sources |
| Plan mode allows mutations | Known bug | [Issue #11115](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues/11115) |
| Forgetting AGENTS.md instructions | Context limits | Keep instructions concise; use skill files for detailed procedures |
| Stalling in Read Only mode | Known issue | [Discussion #7380](https://github.com/openai/codex/discussions/7380) |

### Error Messages Reference

| Error Message | Meaning | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Error: EACCES permission denied` | Sandbox blocked a file operation | Check sandbox mode; use `workspace-write` if Codex needs to edit files |
| `Error: rate limit exceeded` | API rate limit hit | Wait and retry; reduce `model_reasoning_effort` or switch to a lighter model |
| `Error: context length exceeded` | Conversation exceeded 272K input tokens | Use `/compact` to summarize, or start a new session with `/new` |
| `Error: MCP server failed to start` | MCP server process crashed or timed out | Check `codex mcp get <name>` for config; increase `startup_timeout_sec` |
| `Error: authentication required` | No valid API key or session | Run `codex login` or set `CODEX_API_KEY` |
| `Error: sandbox execution failed` | Command failed inside sandbox | Check command syntax; verify required tools are available in the sandbox environment |
| `WARN: skill not found` | Referenced skill doesn’t exist at expected path | Check `/skills` list; verify skill folder location |
| `Error: wire format mismatch` | Wrong `wire_api` setting for provider | Use `wire_api = "responses"` for OpenAI endpoints (see [Custom Model Providers](#which-model-should-i-choose)) |

### What Does `codex doctor` Do?

`codex doctor` is the single command to run before filing a bug or debugging a broken
install. It captures the local runtime, configuration, auth, terminal, network, and state
in one report, and the project’s own issue template asks you to paste its output.[114](#fn:197)

    codex doctor                           # Full human-readable report (the default)
    codex doctor --summary                 # Compact view
    codex doctor --json                    # Redacted structured JSON, for bug reports
    codex doctor --all                     # Expand truncated lists
    codex doctor --no-color                # Strip ANSI color

The report is organized into stable sections, so you can skim to the one you need:[114](#fn:197)

| Section | What it reports |
| --- | --- |
| **Notes** | Anomalies promoted to the top: available updates, oversized rollout directories, optional MCP problems, mixed auth signals |
| **Environment** | Runtime provenance, install consistency, search-tool readiness, terminal/multiplexer metadata, database health |
| **Configuration** | `config.toml` load status, auth details, MCP server config, sandbox policy, feature flags |
| **Updates** | Whether update configuration is locally consistent |
| **Connectivity** | Network environment, WebSocket diagnostics, provider-aware endpoint reachability |
| **Background Server** | App-server daemon status |

Each check carries a status marker – `✓` ok, `✗` fail, `⚠` warn, `○` idle – with a summary
line. `--json` keys every check by a stable identifier, which makes it usable in CI or a
support script rather than only by eye.[114](#fn:197)

The command has grown across releases: it landed in v0.131.0, gained environment, Git,
terminal, app-server, and thread-inventory reporting in v0.135.0, and added editor and pager
details (with sensitive values redacted in JSON) in v0.139.0.[96](#fn:173)[120](#fn:179)[116](#fn:183)
> **Use it first, not last.** Most “Codex is broken” reports resolve to something `doctor` already names: a stale auth mode, a `config.toml` that failed to parse, or a second install
> shadowing the one on your `PATH`.

### Diagnostic Tools

    codex doctor                           # Full local diagnostic (start here)
    codex --version                        # Check CLI version
    codex login status                     # Verify authentication
    codex mcp list                         # Check MCP server status
    codex debug app-server --help          # Debug app server issues

In-session TUI diagnostics:

    /status                                # Token/session overview
    /config                                # Inspect effective config values and sources
    /compact                               # Summarize history to reclaim context
> **Note**: `codex --verbose` is **not** a valid top-level flag. Use the debug subcommands and TUI diagnostics above.

### Clean Reinstall

    npm uninstall -g @openai/codex && npm install -g @openai/codex@latest

Preserves `~/.codex/` (auth, config, sessions). For pinning to an exact version, downgrading
after a bad release, or a full state wipe, see [Update, Downgrade, or Uninstall](#how-do-i-update-downgrade-or-uninstall-the-codex-cli).

### Debug Mode

    codex debug app-server send-message-v2  # Test app-server client

### Reporting Issues

    /feedback                              # Send logs to Codex maintainers (in TUI)

Or file issues at [github.com/openai/codex/issues](https://github.com/openai/codex/issues).[1](#fn:1)

