83. [Introducing GPT-5.5](https://openai.com/index/introducing-gpt-5-5/). OpenAI announcement, April 23, 2026. **Context window: 400K in Codex, 1M in the API** (per OpenAI’s GPT-5.5-in-Codex availability page and the GPT-5.5 API model docs respectively). Pricing (API): $5 input / $30 output per MTok (2× the GPT-5.4 rate; OpenAI states ~20% effective increase after token-efficiency improvements). Benchmarks: **82.7% Terminal-Bench 2.0** (current SOTA across publicly available models), 84.9% GDPval (44 occupations), 78.7% OSWorld-Verified (real-computer operation), 98.0% Tau2-bench Telecom (no prompt tuning). Available in Codex CLI/web/desktop on April 23 for ChatGPT Plus/Pro/Business/Enterprise/Edu/Go; available in the OpenAI API on April 24. OpenAI’s stated guidance: “GPT-5.5 is now available in Codex as OpenAI’s newest frontier model for complex coding, computer use, knowledge work, and research workflows, and is the recommended choice for most Codex tasks.” See also: [Introducing upgrades to Codex](https://openai.com/index/introducing-upgrades-to-codex/) (April 17, 2026 — background computer use, 90+ new plugin partners including Atlassian Rovo, CircleCI, CodeRabbit, GitLab Issues, Microsoft Suite, Neon by Databricks, Remotion, Render, and Superpowers); [NVIDIA blog: GPT-5.5 Powers Codex on NVIDIA Infrastructure](https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/openai-codex-gpt-5-5-ai-agents/); [TechCrunch: OpenAI releases GPT-5.5](https://techcrunch.com/2026/04/23/openai-chatgpt-gpt-5-5-ai-model-superapp/). [↩](#fnref:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")[↩](#fnref2:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")[↩](#fnref3:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")[↩](#fnref4:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")[↩](#fnref5:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")[↩](#fnref6:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")[↩](#fnref7:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")[↩](#fnref8:160 "Jump back to footnote 83 in the text")

84. [Codex CLI v0.122.0](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.122.0) and [v0.123.0](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.123.0). v0.122.0: filesystem deny-read glob policies + managed deny-read requirements + platform sandbox enforcement + isolated `codex exec` runs that ignore user config or rules; tool discovery and image generation enabled by default; higher-detail image handling and original-detail metadata for MCP and `js_repl`; app-server stale-prompt resolution across clients; resumed/forked threads replay token usage immediately; remote-control startup tolerates missing ChatGPT auth; MCP startup cancellation works again through app-server sessions; internal split into `codex-core-plugins` and connector reorg. v0.123.0 (April 23, 2026): built-in `amazon-bedrock` model provider with configurable AWS profile support; `/mcp verbose` for full MCP server diagnostics, resources, and resource templates while keeping plain `/mcp` fast; plugin MCP loading accepts both `mcpServers` and top-level server maps in `.mcp.json`; realtime handoffs deliver transcript deltas to background agents and allow explicit silent-stay; host-specific `remote_sandbox_config` requirements for remote environments; refreshed bundled model metadata. Fixes: `/copy` after rollback copies latest visible assistant response (not pre-rollback), follow-up text submitted while a manual shell command runs is queued (no more stuck `Working` states), Unicode/dead-key input in VS Code WSL terminals (enhanced keyboard mode disabled there), stale proxy env vars not restored from shell snapshots, `codex exec` inherits root-level shared flags such as sandbox and model options, leaked review prompts removed from TUI transcripts. [↩](#fnref:161 "Jump back to footnote 84 in the text")[↩](#fnref2:161 "Jump back to footnote 84 in the text")[↩](#fnref3:161 "Jump back to footnote 84 in the text")

85. [Codex CLI v0.124.0](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.124.0) and [v0.125.0](https://github.com/openai/codex/releases/tag/rust-v0.125.0). v0.124.0 (April 23, 2026): TUI quick reasoning controls (`Alt+,` lower, `Alt+.` raise) with model-upgrade reasoning resets to new model’s defaults; app-server sessions manage multiple environments with per-turn environment + working-directory selection for remote setups; first-class Amazon Bedrock support for OpenAI-compatible providers (AWS SigV4 signing, AWS credential auth); remote plugin marketplaces with reliable detail lookups and larger result pages; **hooks now stable** — configurable inline in `config.toml` and `requirements.toml`, observe MCP tools plus `apply_patch` and long-running Bash sessions; eligible ChatGPT plans default to the **Fast service tier** unless explicitly opted out. Fixes: Cloudflare cookies preserved across approved ChatGPT hosts (auth-failure reduction), websocket event draining under load + cleaner shutdown, permission-mode drift survives side conversations, `wait_agent` returns promptly when mailbox work is queued, local stdio MCP launches for relative commands without explicit `cwd` use correct path resolution, startup managed-config edge cases (unknown feature requirements warn instead of abort, cloud-requirement errors are clearer). v0.125.0 (April 24, 2026): app-server integrations support Unix socket transport, pagination-friendly resume/fork, sticky environments, remote thread config/store plumbing; app-server plugin management installs remote plugins and upgrades configured marketplaces; permission profiles round-trip across TUI sessions, user turns, MCP sandbox state, shell escalation, and app-server APIs; model providers own model discovery with AWS/Bedrock account state exposed to app clients; `codex exec --json` reports reasoning-token usage for programmatic consumers; rollout tracing records tool, code-mode, session, and multi-agent relationships, with a debug reducer command. Fixes: `/review` interrupt no longer wedges the TUI, exec-server output handling and stream closure improved, app-server respects explicitly untrusted project config, websocket client disconnection issues during notification bursts, Windows sandbox startup and background process handling, config-schema validation strengthened for thread limits, agent paths, and MIME types. [↩](#fnref:162 "Jump back to footnote 85 in the text")[↩](#fnref2:162 "Jump back to footnote 85 in the text")[↩](#fnref3:162 "Jump back to footnote 85 in the text")

